### Robust Array Verification for Reveal Contexts

Using `JSON.stringify` for array comparison is fragile. When validating expected array fields, we should explicitly verify that all expected elements are present (rather than just checking the array length or using order-sensitive comparisons) to prevent silent mismatches. If the field is present but is not an array, we should process it as an empty array to ensure the system fails closed safely.

```javascript
function hasExactRevealContexts(policy) {
  const allowedAfter = policy && Array.isArray(policy.allowed_after) ? policy.allowed_after : [];
  const expected = [
    'submitted_answer',
    'student_confirmed_attempt',
    'teacher_or_admin_preview',
  ];
  return expected.every((val) => allowedAfter.includes(val)) && allowedAfter.length === expected.length;
}
```

